A||||20||theogre||Snake Charming. Check those serpent belts.||||4||
Z||000000||theogre||12-20-2000||08:34 PM||||If the serpent belt is squeaking on the 4 cyl find out why. fast. Mine started squeaking a few days ago. I figured it was just dirt again. Not.<P>Today on my way to work the belt lost a strip of material off one side all the way around the belt. When it did so it beat the hell out of things. Good thing it was only one ridge wide or it likely would have pulled out a cable or cut a hose. ((Oh well, I <I>was</I> undecided about removing the insulation from back there. Now that its a shreded mess it's got to go the next warm day I get.))<P>This is fairly common with serpents. They don't always just break like V belts do. This kind of failure can happen to any serpent application.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Lake Superior? Is that what I stepped in? Sorry.||||reg||4
Z||000001||My7Fieros||12-20-2000||10:09 PM||||Wonder why Fieros (both 4 and 6 cylenders) are so prone to having belt probs? Hmmmm||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cheever2||12-20-2000||10:36 PM||||Ogre, mine only squeals when it gets wet, i.e. rain or car wash.  I haven't worried about it, but it can sure be embarrassing at stop lights.  What should I do?  It's been replaced recently, but the new one does it too.<P>||||reg||6
Z||000003||DaveL||12-21-2000||08:05 AM||||Happened to me once while driving to work. It was a good chance to make sure my idiot lights work, and they did! I was only driving about 35 or so, and I heard a slight noise from the engine compartment, like I had hit something small that got kicked up under the car. The alt. idiot light came on, and very shortly the temp light went on, and the guage started climbimg. I shut down immediately then, and parked it. $80 later, on the road again. (No relation to Willie Nelson) How often would you replace them? Sounds like the sort of thing you might want to do regularly rather than wait till it fails.||||reg||12
Z||000004||theogre||12-21-2000||08:59 PM||||Cheever, 2 things can cause that. Well maybe 3.<P>1. the belt is loose. The tensioning device is weak on a 4 or it's not installed right on a V6.<P>2. one of the devices the belt runs has something wrong with it. Could be a bearing problem or a bad pully.<P>3. could be the new belt is bad. It happens. With Dayco belts it happens allot.<P><BR>My7, I think allot of the short belt life is heat. Fiero engine bays are hot even on cold days. I think they don't move a whole lot of air thru at highway speed. at low speed all you get is convection. You can hit 200+F in there easy. <P>Even at low speeds the FWD cars move air. If they heat up the fan helps cool them down. The egine bay fan on V6 is only there to help the alt and coil. It probly doesn't do much for the belts.<P>Plus on AC 4 cyls the serpent runs right above the exhaust for a good ways. The AC belt on the others is right down there as well. That can't help the situ any. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fiero_Freak||12-23-2000||11:52 AM||||Do you guys have a Tensioner on your v6's?   Mine sure doesn't.  I have a v-type belt on the ac and a serpentine like belt on the rest...  Thanks||||reg||11
Z||000006||theogre||12-23-2000||11:58 AM||||The 2.8l V6 like that is an interim motor. It's in between a full serpent setup and a V setup. I suspect when the V6 is in other cars from that time frame it may have been a full serpent.<P>As it is only 87-88 Fiero with 4 cyl are full serpent with automatic tension. <P>The V6 still uses the alternator to tension the serpent. This presents a problem in that serpent is very critical of tension. It can be a real pain to adjust. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||firstfiero||12-23-2000||01:37 PM||||   can a loose belt cause my alternator to not work properly. Since winter it seems my battery needs charged about every 4 weeks. When I had the alternator tested they said when I turn the heat on with lights its taking a little more then what my alternator is putting out hence the slow discharge. The belt does look old and squeels when I first start the car. The battery place said I need a new alt. but do you think I should just have the belt replace first and go from their?   86 se v6<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||12-23-2000||08:40 PM||||It certainly would not hurt to try that first. If the belt squeals at all it is a problem. Being loose would definatly leave the alternator weak.<P>The belt for the V6 is fairly cheap. you can change it with common tools. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||firstfiero||12-24-2000||10:48 AM|||| Really...It looks like it's a pain in the ass to get to it.   86se||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||12-24-2000||11:06 AM||||I didn't say easy. I said fairly cheap and no special tools needed. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||01-03-2001||08:43 PM||||I've been in contact with Gates rubber about these belts. From the very detailed responce I recieved it looks like the big reason for short serpent life in Fiero, other than abuse or contamination, is likely the heat.<P>They are telling me that our common 200+ under hood temperature is pushing the top end of what most belts are made for. No big suprise.<P>I asked about pulley wrap as well. They told me 60 degrees was about the minimum. Since the water pump and AC pulleys both have around 90 degrees of belt contact they probably are ok.||||reg||1
Z||000012||mgbringo||01-04-2001||09:19 PM||||A broken belt cost me an engine in my 1984 4 cyl.  My 17 year old daughter was driving it and did not notice the gauge.  She drove it until it locked up.  The engine had about 10,000 miles on a new rebuild.<P>Put $2,600 into a new engine and a couple of months got rear ended by a full size Oldsmobile.  Came out alright with insurance after complaining about a sore neck.  It was amazing how badly the Olds was smashed and had to be towed away.  i drove my Fiero home after tying up the dangling bumper.<P>------------------<BR>1986 Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||01-04-2001||09:34 PM||||I've replaced the serp belt in my '86SE with 2.8 3 times now. First one was because of bent w/p pulley. The second worked fine, but slipped everytime I started the engine until I revved it up a couple times. I replaced it with a third thinking that belt was bad. It slips on every startup also. Brand new belt both times. I have been retensioning the belts are about 15 minutes of run time for belt stretch.<P>It's really easy, takes 10 minutes to replace the belt on the 6. <BR><B>Disconnect battery</B><BR>1. Remove the pass rear wheel.<BR>2. From wheel well put a 13mm socket on the alt lock down bolt and loosen it a few turns.<BR>3. Remove old belt, and slide new belt onto pulleys.<BR>4. Put 13 socket back on the lock down nut and tighten it until just before the alt locks down.<BR>5. From the open wheel well I use an 18" jack handle and stick it between the alt housing and the engine mount bracket. Pry against the alt to put tension on the belt, and tighten the lock down nut with the other hand. Works real slick, and takes just a second. Be careful or you might break the cheap cast alt housing. I haven't yet, but don't want anyone to break theirs because of my suggestions.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Racer 1||01-04-2001||11:03 PM||||You all sound like a bunch of cry babies. CRY CRY CRY geez. If you over tighten the belt it will squeel 1/2 slack test. One time on my 2.5 the bottom bolt came out and the alt.The alt kept on moving foward loosing up the belt. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://spitfire.cwv.net/~cdbailey/images/gifssucl/eyes2/evil_eyes.gif"> ||||reg||8
Z||000015||theogre||01-05-2001||06:22 PM||||Obviouslly you don't know a thing about serpents on the 4 cyl. It is an automatic tension system. There is no adjustment. The issue at hand is short belt life on otherwise good engines. This thread come out of a developing cave article.<P>Well... first off this thread was started about checking serpents as soon as they start squeaking. Once that starts, the belt is not long for the world. Serpents will not take slipping for as long as V belts usually do before breaking.<P>The rest is concerning the short life of belts on otherwise good engines. It is mainly an 87-88 4 cylinder thread but the Gates information I have applies to all serpent or V type belts. My Fiero isn't the only one I've worked on. I've been in the car and computer business for over 20 years. Belt problems in Fiero are very common. Worse than any other car I've seen. Belt problems are one of the top issues in this forum. <P>The 87 & up 4 cylinder serpent CANNOT be over tightened or loose unless there is something wrong. The tensioning device is calibrated to make sure of this.<P>In My car…. The tensioning device water pump and alternator have all been replaced. The pulleys are good and alignment has been checked. There's no grease or oil getting on it. My AC is dead so there are no shock loads from compressor cycling. The belt still won't last more than a year. At $20-30 each for serpents, this gets rather expensive.<P>No matter how carefully belts are tightened on the older L4 or the V6 neither V nor Serpent belts last more than a year or so in many cases. Too many cases to be attributed only to tension problems. Worn sheaves don't account for this either. Alignment problems are possible but rare on most stock motors. <P>These same motors in other cars don't have this level of belt problems. In most other cars, belts last 3-5 years or more even in extreme driving. Many Grand Am and Cutlass Calais from 88-91 still have their original belt on. That leaves it to crappy replacement belts or operating conditions as being major causes of rapid belt failures. I've tried multiple brands of belts so that eliminates belt quality.<P>I asked Gates Rubber Co. what to look for beyond the obvious. I specifically asked about under hood temperatures and how much of the pulley should be covered by the serpent. In order to eliminate any possible bias about car types, I asked them the questions as they would relate to performance applications where you often have high heat and a need to alter belt routes. <P>I asked about pulley wrap because the last belt started squeaking on the water pump pulley just before it let go. This pulley has only about 90 degrees of wrap but in other cars with this engine, that pulley has 120-180 degrees of wrap and usually runs on the smooth side of the belt.<P>Gates recommended a minimum 60 degree wrap on loaded pulleys so that pretty much eliminates the possibility that a good water pump is causing rapid belt wear.<P>That leaves heat. I've actually measured engine bay temperatures of a running car driving around town. At low speed, the engine bay easily reaches 150F+ on a 70F day. It can hit 200F+ regularly. Gate's response was that at 200F and above, the belt life would degrade considerably. Since I am commonly stuck in slow traffic I likely spend quite allot of time on the high end of the temperature range compared to someone doing mostly highway driving where there is much better cooling of the engine bay.<P>This is just one of the ongoing issues caused by engine bay heat. Little of this should surprise anyone. It's well established what Fiero engine bay heat does to many other parts but until now, we had no information about the belts beyond installation instructions.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000016||fiero56||01-05-2001||06:58 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-05-2001).]||||reg||10
